/// Questions drawn from Sets 11 to 14 of the same classic school-quiz book.
///
/// Same approach as the three banks before it, and for the same reasons: the
/// source is in copyright and its rounds are open-ended or three-option,
/// while this game needs four. What is taken is the subject matter and the
/// answer -- facts, which belong to nobody -- and the wording, the options
/// and every distractor are written fresh. Nothing is reproduced verbatim.
///
/// Left out on the way in:
///
///   * Set 12 asks which island Marco Polo called the 'Female Island' and
///     answers Minicoy. Which island he meant is genuinely disputed, so the
///     question is not asked.
///
///   * Set 14 credits Job Charnock with founding Calcutta. A Calcutta High
///     Court bench rejected that in 2003 and the city has no official
///     founder. The question survives here as what Charnock was
///     traditionally credited with, which is true and is the part he would
///     have been taught.
///
///   * Set 11 item 2 explains gold's symbol Au as coming from Aurora,
///     goddess of dawn. Aurum's own origin is older and argued over, so the
///     question asks only what Au stands for.
///
///   * One question repeated a calcium oxide item already in an earlier
///     bank, checked against all 2,207 questions already there, and was
///     dropped rather than asked twice.
///
/// Answer positions cycle 0, 1, 2, 3 rather than sitting wherever they were
/// drafted. This screen does not shuffle its options, and a position that is
/// right more often than the others is a way to score without reading the
/// question.
